{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Clean up Your Container Images with Amazon ECR Lifecycle Policies | AWS Compute Blog","context":"## Ground Rules\n\nOne of the biggest benefits of deploying code in containers is the ability to quickly and easily roll back to a previous version. You can deploy with less risk because, if something goes wrong, it is easy to revert back to the previous container version and know that your application will run like it did before the failed deployment. Most people probably never roll back past a few versions. If your situation is similar, then one simple lifecycle rule might be to just keep the last 30 images.\n\n### Last 30 Images\n\nIn your ECR registry, choose **Dry-Run Lifecycle Rules, Add**.\n\n* For **Image Status**, select **Untagged**.\n* Under **Match criteria**, for **Count Type**, enter **Image Count More Than**.\n* For **Count Number**, enter **30**.\n* For **Rule action**, choose **expire**.\n\nChoose **Save**. To see which images would be cleaned up, **Save and dry-run rules**.\n\nOf course, there are teams who, for compliance reasons, might prefer to keep certain images for a period of time, rather than keeping by count. For that situation, you can choose to clean up images older than 90 days.\n\n### Last 90 Days\n\nSelect the rule that you just created and choose Edit. Change the parameters to keep only 90 days of untagged images:\n\n* Under **Match criteria**, for **Count Type**, enter **Since Image Pushed**\n* For **Count Number**, enter **90**.\n* For **Count Unit**, enter **days**","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/clean-up-your-container-images-with-amazon-ecr-lifecycle-policies/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Lifecycle policy properties in Amazon ECR","context":"## Count number\n\n`countNumber`\n: Type: integer\n\n Required: yes\n\n Specify a count number. Acceptable values are positive integers\n (`0` is not an accepted value).\n\n If the `countType` used is `imageCountMoreThan`,\n then the value is the maximum number of images that you want to retain\n in your repository. If the `countType` used is\n `sinceImagePushed`, then the value is the maximum age\n limit for your images. If the `countType` used is `sinceImagePulled`, then the value is the maximum number of days since the image was last pulled. If the `countType` used is `sinceImageTransitioned`, then the value is the maximum number of days since the image was archived","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonECR/latest/userguide/lifecycle_policy_parameters.html"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Rule Lifecycle API","context":"## Response body fields\n\n| Field | Data type | Description |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `id` | String | The unique identifier for the rule. |\n| `description` | String | The explanation or purpose of the rule. |\n| `updated_at` | String | The timestamp of the most recent update to the rule in UTC format. |\n| `<attribute_key>` | Array | The attribute values used to match incoming queries. |\n| `<feature_type>` | String | The value assigned to the feature type if the rule matches. |\n| `search_after` | Array | The token for paginating additional results. Present only if more results exist. |","url":"https://docs.opensearch.org/docs/3.1/tuning-your-cluster/availability-and-recovery/rule-based-autotagging/rule-lifecycle-api/"}]}}

## Recent AWS announcements (What's New)

Newer than your training data may be. When these conflict with the documentation results above or with what you remember, prefer the most recent dated source.

{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon ECR introduces archive storage class for rarely accessed container images - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Container Registry (ECR) has introduced a new archive storage class to help reduce storage costs for infrequently accessed container images. This new feature allows users to meet compliance and retention requirements while optimizing storage expenses. ECR's lifecycle policies now support archiving images based on last pull time, enabling automatic archiving based on usage patterns. Users can also archive images individually using the ECR Console or API. Archived images do not count towards the image per repository limit and are inaccessible for pulls but can be easily restored within 20 minutes via the ECR Console, CLI, or API. All archival and restore operations are logged through CloudTrail for auditability. The new ECR archive storage class is available in all AWS Commercial and AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. For pricing details, visit the AWS pricing page. To learn more, consult the documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/11/amazon-ecr-archive-storage-class-container-images/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"EC2 Image Builder enhances lifecycle policies with wildcard support and simplified IAM - AWS","context":"EC2 Image Builder, a service offered by AWS for automating the creation, distribution, and management of customized Amazon Machine Images, has introduced two new features to simplify image management. Firstly, wildcard patterns are now supported in lifecycle policies, allowing users to manage images from multiple recipes within a single policy. This feature automatically applies lifecycle policies to all matching recipes, including new ones created in the future. Secondly, IAM role creation for lifecycle management has been simplified, with the console now automatically populating the required default permissions when creating a new role. Previously, users had to create separate lifecycle policies for each new recipe or manually configure permissions, making it difficult to manage image lifecycles at scale. These enhancements reduce setup time and potential configuration errors, enabling users to manage their image lifecycle more efficiently. Lifecycle Policies are available in all commercial AWS regions","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/02/ec2-image-builder-lifecycle-enhancements/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"aws-resilience-lifecycle","skill_description":"Guides the end-to-end AWS resilience lifecycle integrating Resilience Hub v2, Fault Injection Service, and Application Recovery Controller. Covers the Define → Test → Operate workflow: from policy creation through failure mode assessment, to FIS experiment validation, to ARC operational controls. Applicable when the user wants a complete resilience strategy, needs to connect findings to experiments to controls, or is planning a resilience program. Also applicable for the meta question of whether marking NGRH findings as resolved is enough, whether they are \"done\" after resolving findings, or how to validate findings before resolving them. Not applicable for resolving or remediating a specific individual finding (see resilience-hub-failure-mode-assessment), or when a single service is explicitly named (e.g. \"what FIS experiment should I run\").\n\n\nServices: resiliencehub, fis, route53-recovery-control-config, arc-zonal-shift\nTasks: configure, test, audit\nPersona: architect, sre, devops\nWorkload: resilience\n*","skill_name":"aws-resilience-lifecycle"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"recovery-controller-setup","skill_description":"Configures AWS Application Recovery Controller (ARC) for operational resilience: routing controls with safety rules for cross-Region failover, and zonal shift / zonal autoshift for AZ-impairment recovery. Applies when setting up failover routing, configuring safety rules, enabling zonal shift, or configuring zonal autoshift with practice runs. Also applies when shifting traffic out of a specific Availability Zone (AZ) for an ALB/NLB or other resource. For a broader \"an AZ is impaired, what is my response across services\" question, see aws-resilience-lifecycle. Does not apply to Resilience Hub setup or FIS experiments.\n\n\nServices: route53-recovery-control-config, arc-zonal-shift\nTasks: configure, deploy\nPersona: developer, devops, sre\nWorkload: resilience\n*","skill_name":"recovery-controller-setup"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"AWS Config now supports 191 additional managed rules - AWS","context":"AWS Config has added support for 191 new managed rules across various services including Amazon Bedrock, Amazon SageMaker, Amazon ECS, Amazon EKS, Amazon RDS, Amazon Redshift, Amazon S3, and Amazon CloudTrail. These rules cover encryption, logging, public access, network security, data protection, and operational best practices. Users can deploy these rules individually or as part of a conformance pack. The new rules include ACM_CERTIFICATE_RSA_CHECK for AWS Certificate Manager, API_GWV2_ACCESS_LOGS_ENABLED for Amazon API Gateway, APPSYNC_AUTHORIZATION_CHECK and APPSYNC_LOGGING_ENABLED for AWS AppSync, and many more for other services. The expansion increases built-in governance coverage across AI workloads and core cloud infrastructure","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/07/aws-config-additional-managed-rules/"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify ECR lifecycle policy rule selection criteria via AWS documentation."}
